<title>USA: U.S. Oct producer prices rose 0.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Oct producer prices rose 0.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> Finished Goods		0.4    0.2     3.0</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 -0.3    0.3     0.8</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	     0.8    0.2     5.0</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 1.9    0.2     9.5</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.5  131.6     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     5.5    0.9    21.7</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		  3.8    2.0    41.9</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   0.3   -1.0     2.4</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	    -1.6    1.2    -0.1</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	 -0.4    0.3     0.4</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 Unch    0.3     0.5</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 -0.1    0.1    -1.6</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	-0.2    0.2    -2.0</p>
<p> -Construction Materials  -0.3    0.4     1.1</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Oct   Sept   Oct96/95</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    1.7    0.8    10.3</p>
<p> Crude Goods		  -0.6   -1.5     9.4</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	0.3    0.6    -7.8</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -2.7   -3.8     8.9</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	  1.1    0.6     9.8</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	 1.5    0.6    23.6</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    8.5    7.3    48.4</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast October producer prices to increase by 0.2 percent. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.1 percent rise.</p>
<p>The department said the increase in the overall PPI for October was the biggest since a 0.5 percent rise in March 1996. It also said that PPI excluding food and energy posted its sharpest decline since -0.4 percent in October 1994.</p>
<p>Passenger car prices in October registered the steepest decline since October 1994, when they fell 1.8 percent.</p>
